{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "deposited_count",
      "description": "The cumulative number of requested-color cubes released from the gripper into the bin during execution. Initially 0. Increment at the first act cycle whose incoming motion includes the completed release of a carried requested-color cube above the bin, not at the cycle that merely chooses the opening command. Count the release even when the cube disappears into the dark bin and its final resting position is invisible. A cycle containing both release and retreat counts once. Do not count grasps, transfers with the gripper closed, newly appearing tabletop cubes, or subsequent views of an already completed release. Retain the count through retreat and button pressing. e.g. 2",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "deposited_count",
      "description": "Initialize to 0 when memory is empty. Otherwise carry the incoming count forward. Add one when this cycle's motion frames show a requested-color cube carried over the bin being released as the fingers open, with the current hand empty. This includes a motion that also retreats after opening. The release must occur in the incoming motion: an already empty open hand above the bin is not another release. Do not increment for a command you are about to issue, a grasp, a closed-hand transfer, or new cubes appearing on the table. If no steps executed, retain the count unchanged. Keep the count after reaching the requested total and throughout button pressing. e.g. 2"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Read the requested color and quantity N from the goal each cycle. Use deposited_count plus any release visibly completed in the current incoming motion to determine how many deposits are now complete; do not count a release twice. Start with an open hand and zero deposits. Infer object positions from calibrated front and wrist views, using the tabletop plane and cube geometry rather than treating image coordinates as world coordinates. Cubes are approximately 4 cm wide. Locate the bin by its opening and the stop button by its gray circular top on a light square base. Re-estimate positions from current views; scene objects can appear during execution. Robot right is negative world y.\n\nWhen fewer than N cubes have been deposited and the hand is empty away from the bin's release position, select a visible, accessible requested-color tabletop cube. Prefer a clear grasp and maintain the selection when the hand is already aligned above a cube; do not switch to another cube simply because new cubes appear. Approach its center at z=0.15 with the gripper open, roll=-3.1416, pitch=0, and yaw aligned with a cube edge, choosing the equivalent square orientation within roughly [-0.7854,0.7854]. When already above that cube near z=0.15, descend to its center at z=0.02 and close. Use the wrist view to refine the same cube's center rather than descending at an inaccurate reported approach position.\n\nWhen a cube is grasped at table height, lift vertically to z=0.15, retaining its grasp orientation and keeping the gripper closed. A cube between the fingers and finger positions around 0.018 m support a successful grasp. Once lifted, move to the world xy center of the bin opening at z=0.20, keeping the cube closed in the gripper and preserving the current orientation. When the carried cube is above the bin at release height, command open at the centered release position. This is a separate operation from the closed-hand transfer. If the target would be identical to the current TCP position, add a 0.003 m vertical displacement so opening executes.\n\nWhen the hand is empty and open at the bin after a release, retreat toward the robot: target [bin_x-0.10, bin_y, 0.20], preserving orientation and keeping open. Perform this retreat even after the final requested cube. If the incoming motion already included this retreat and the hand is at that offset, do not repeat it. Retreat clears the opening and gives the dropped cube time to fall; no separate arbitrary wait is needed.\n\nWhen N deposits are complete and the release retreat is finished, ignore all remaining cubes. Approach the button center at z=0.15, roll=-3.1416, pitch=0, yaw=0, with the gripper open. When already aligned above the button with open fingers, close the empty gripper at that approach position, adding a 0.003 m vertical displacement if needed to ensure a nonzero motion. When aligned above the button with closed fingers, descend to [button_x, button_y, 0.0075] with the gripper closed. If the current motion shows this descent reaching button contact, retract to [button_x, button_y, 0.15] with the gripper still closed. Contact can leave the measured TCP near z=0.0245 rather than the commanded depth; recognize the visible contact and retract instead of repeatedly demanding a deeper press.\n\nFor an unanticipated or ambiguous situation, use the visible hand contents, finger opening, height, and object alignment to recover the applicable rule. Never increase the deposit count without an observed release. If a grasp fails, reopen with a small nonzero upward motion and re-approach a visible requested-color cube. If no suitable cube is currently available before the quota is reached, wait 16 control steps specifically to observe newly arriving cubes, then reassess. If an object needed for localization is occluded, move upward to a clear viewing height while retaining any held cube, then re-localize. Never press the stop button before the requested number of deposits."
}
